# ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING — read before editing this file.
#
# git applies the LAST matching pattern for a given attribute, so the
# catch-all `* text=auto` MUST come first and every specific override MUST
# follow it. In `D:\Dev\pdfcer` this file once had the catch-all sitting in
# the middle, below the `*.pdf binary` rule, and the catch-all silently
# WON for .pdf: `git check-attr text -- foo.pdf` reported `text: auto`,
# not `unset`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, AND WHY IT WAS WRITTEN BEFORE THE FIRST
# COMMIT RATHER THAN AFTER
# =====================================================================
#
# `core.autocrlf` is **true** globally on this machine. Under that
# setting, a repository with no `.gitattributes` normalizes line endings
# in anything git guesses is text — and git guesses by looking for NUL
# bytes, which a small synthetic PDF may not contain.
#
# The damage that caused in pdfcer (found 2026-08-02, in a fresh worktree):
#
#   - On checkout, 69 binary fixtures were CRLF-inflated. A PDF's
#     cross-reference table stores ABSOLUTE BYTE OFFSETS, so every offset
#     past the first rewrite pointed at the wrong byte and the file failed
#     to parse.
#   - Worse, four fixtures were damaged **in the index itself**, because
#     git stripped their CRs at `git add` time rather than at checkout.
#     Two of those exist SPECIFICALLY to test CRLF handling in
#     cross-reference sections (ISO 32000-1 §7.5.4 requires exactly
#     20-byte xref entries) — normalization turned six entries into 19
#     bytes, so the very bytes under test were the bytes destroyed.
#
# Neither state worked, and the main tree only kept passing because it
# still held the original on-disk bytes and had never been re-checked-out
# — which is the property that makes this class of damage invisible until
# someone clones.
#
# That last point is why this file predates the first commit here. The
# initial `git add` of this workspace was made WITHOUT it, noticed, and
# unwound with `git rm -r --cached` before anything was committed,
# precisely because the pdfcer finding says the corruption can land at add
# time. There was no history to lose at that point; there would have been
# five minutes later.
#
# So: catch-all first, specific rules after. Do not reorder.
* text=auto

# Force LF for anything that must run as a script or gets parsed
# line-sensitively, regardless of the checkout platform's autocrlf
# setting — a CRLF-mangled shebang script fails to run. `tools/gates/*.sh`
# are run by `run-all.sh` through bash on Windows, so this is not
# hypothetical here.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.ron text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Markdown/docs and the mockups: normalize to LF for consistent diffs.
# Content does not depend on it, but mixed line endings make diffs noisy,
# and this project's documents are large and reviewed often.
*.md text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf

# The icon assets. LF rather than `binary`, because they ARE text and a
# readable diff on a glyph's path data is worth having — but pinned,
# because two properties depend on their exact bytes:
#
#   * they are `include_str!`-embedded, so a line ending is compiled into
#     the program and moves `package-portable.py`'s source digest; and
#   * they were verified byte-identical to `D:\Dev\pdfcer`'s copies with
#     `cmp` at salvage time, and a checkout that silently inflated them to
#     CRLF would end that property without touching a single glyph.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Binary fixtures and evidence: NEVER apply end-of-line normalization.
# Marked explicitly so `* text=auto` above can never misdetect a NUL-free
# synthetic PDF as text and mangle it. (`binary` == `-text -diff`.)
#
# The .png rules matter as much as the .pdf one here: `evidence/` is 18
# screenshots that back observational claims in README.md's evidence
# index. A normalized PNG is a corrupt PNG, and the claim it backs
# silently loses its evidence.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.bin binary
*.ico binary
# ★ `.rgba` — a RAW pixel buffer, and the reason it is listed rather than left
# to `text=auto` is this file's own opening argument.
#
# `crates/pdfcer-gui/assets/window-icon-64.rgba` is 64x64x4 bytes of straight
# RGBA handed to `eframe::IconData`, which requires the length to be EXACTLY
# `width * height * 4`. Git's binary heuristic samples the first 8,000 bytes
# for a NUL and would very likely get this right — the icon is mostly
# transparent, so the buffer is mostly zeroes — but "very likely" is what the
# 69 CRLF-inflated fixtures at the top of this file were too.
#
# The failure would also be quiet in a new way: a CRLF-inflated buffer is
# LONGER than its declared dimensions, winit rejects it with a log line nobody
# reads, and the window icon silently stops appearing on a fresh clone while
# the executable's own icon resource keeps working. Two different mechanisms
# for one picture, one of them broken, on someone else's machine.
*.rgba binary

# Windows resource scripts. Pinned to LF with the `.rs`/`.toml` family rather
# than left to `text=auto`, for the same reason they are: `pdfcer-gui.rc` is
# compiled by `rc.exe` during the build, so its bytes reach a build product,
# and `package-portable.py` digests the source tree.
*.rc text eol=lf

# Font binaries — same rationale as the PDF-fixture rules above.
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.cff binary
*.pfb binary
